WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
React Native 0.76 activates the new architecture by default.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.000
After years of opt-in testing,

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.000
the framework now locks into a new baseline

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
for how JavaScript communicates with native code.

5
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.000
For most engineering teams,

6
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
the core framework itself upgrades cleanly.

7
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.000
The internal React Native changes are extensively tested

8
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
and rarely cause a build to fail.

9
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
The single largest source of friction

10
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.000
and the reason most migration stall

11
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.000
is the third-party dependency tree

12
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Before committing to an upgrade timeline

13
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
or writing a single line of feature code,

14
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000
teams must halt and execute a complete structural audit

15
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.000
of their package.json.

16
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Moving to 0.76 is a dependency triage exercise.

17
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The success of the upgrade depends entirely

18
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
on identifying which libraries natively support the new architecture

19
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
and which will block your release.

20
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.000
You will encounter two distinct categories of compatibility problems.

21
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.000
The first is a functional failure,

22
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
a library that relies on deprecated native APIs

23
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
and lacks new architecture support for version 0.76.

24
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.000
The second is a superficial metadata mismatch.

25
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.000
A library might be structurally perfectly compatible

26
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.000
with the new architecture,

27
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.000
but its package configuration declares a strict peer dependency

28
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.000
on React Native 0.74.

29
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
When you run your install command on 0.76,

30
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000
the package manager rejects it.

31
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
One is a code failure, requiring a structural fix

32
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.000
or complete replacement.

33
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.000
The other is an artificial blockade enforced by a package manager.

34
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Distinguishing a functional breakage from a rigid metadata rule

35
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
prevents engineering teams from burning days

36
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
attempting to rewrite libraries that simply need an installation override.

37
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
With a typical enterprise application carrying 50 or more dependencies,

38
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000
guessing line by line is inefficient.

39
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The inventory phase requires bulk compatibility checking.

40
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
You can scan your local repository using the React Native Checked New Archie CLI tool.

41
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.000
It cross-references your current installation

42
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.000
against known compatibility lists.

43
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Alternatively, you can use the web-based React Native package checker.

44
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
By pasting your entire package.json dependency block,

45
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.000
you generate a bulk output table detailing the precise support status

46
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.000
and last update for every library in your project.

47
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
For any ambiguous results, cross-reference the package

48
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.000
in the React Native directory.

49
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Filter explicitly by the new architecture supported badge.

50
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
This data is self-reported by library maintainers

51
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.000
and provides the most reliable signal of ongoing support.

52
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
By utilizing these scanning tools,

53
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
an unpredictable monolithic upgrade is broken down

54
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
into a quantifiable engineering checklist.

55
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.000
This diagram outlines the triage algorithm,

56
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.000
sorting dependencies into three buckets.

57
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
We start with Scenario A.

58
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Actively maintained, fully compatible libraries.

59
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Most active packages fall here, requiring just a version bump.

60
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
However, Scenario A often requires crossing strict major version boundaries.

61
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
React Native reanimated requires exactly version three.

62
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Version two will crash.

63
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Similarly, React Native screens must reach version 3.29

64
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
and React Native gesture handler requires version 2.21

65
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.000
or higher to render correctly on the new fabric layer.

66
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Processing the Scenario A library's first

67
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.000
clears the bulk of the dependency tree noise,

68
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.000
allowing you to isolate the specific nodes requiring manual intervention.

69
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.000
The next bucket is Scenario B.

70
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.000
These are libraries that still rely on legacy bridge mechanisms,

71
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.000
like native modules or native emitter event.

72
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.000
When running these libraries on 0.76,

73
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
the Metro Bundler will surface bright red deprecation warnings.

74
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.000
A common misconception is that these warnings mean the library is completely broken

75
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.000
and requires immediate replacement.

76
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Look at this architectural schematic.

77
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.000
When a Scenario B library calls the absent legacy bridge,

78
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
a C++ middle tier called the interrupt layer intercepts that call.

79
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.000
It transparently translates the legacy API request

80
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.000
and routes it directly into the new turbo modules or fabric architecture.

81
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.000
React navigation version six is a prime example.

82
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.000
While navigation version seven is natively compatible,

83
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:14.000
version six functions reliably on 0.76 entirely through this interrupt mechanism

84
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.000
despite generating extensive terminal warnings.

85
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
The operational rule for Scenario B is straightforward.

86
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.000
If the specific feature works on device,

87
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.000
do not panic disable the new architecture.

88
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.000
File an issue or a pull request with the library maintainer to migrate to turbo modules

89
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.000
and keep moving.

90
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Deprecation warnings for these interrupt libraries represent technical debt

91
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.000
you need to address in the future, not an immediate blocker for your 0.76 release.

92
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.000
The final bucket is Scenario C.

93
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.000
These libraries are either completely abandoned

94
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.000
or functionally incompatible with the new native layers.

95
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.000
This failure happens because the libraries contain deep native integrations,

96
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.000
custom C++ code, or direct JSI implementations.

97
00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.000
These bypass the interrupt layer entirely.

98
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.000
If the bridge is gone, they crash the build.

99
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.000
This table shows hard replacements for abandoned packages.

100
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Legacy libraries must be swapped for modern alternatives.

101
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.000
For example, react native camera is replaced by vision camera

102
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.000
and old Firebase implementations require version 21 or higher.

103
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Alternatively, if a fully compatible library is blocked solely by a strict 0.74 peer dependency mismatch,

104
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.000
you can use dash dash legacy peer depths or package manager overrides to force the installation.

105
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Scenario C requires decisive action.

106
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.000
You either surgically replace dead code with modern equivalents

107
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.000
or forcefully override arbitrary package manager restrictions.

108
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.000
Occasionally, a team will hit a hard wall,

109
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.000
a single, deeply integrated, un-maintained legacy dependency

110
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.000
that blocks the entire production release with no immediate replacement available.

111
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.000
In this exact scenario, execute the fallback protocol.

112
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:01.000
Complete the upgrade to react native 0.76 to capture the core framework improvements,

113
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:06.000
but explicitly disable the new architecture flag for that specific release.

114
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.000
This is a temporary measure.

115
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Disabling the flag incurs immediate technical debt.

116
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.000
You must set a strict internal deadline to replace that blocking library

117
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.000
and reactivate the new architecture.

118
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:23.000
A single outdated package should not freeze your organization's entire application framework upgrade cycle.

119
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.000
Zooming out from the individual package files,

120
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.000
the broader react native ecosystem has already fully crossed the tipping point.

121
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.000
Over 61% of the top 400 most installed react native libraries currently support the new architecture,

122
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.000
and that metric increases steadily every month.

123
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.000
You can monitor ongoing support for any remaining holdouts in your project

124
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.000
by tracking the official react working group library status tracker.

125
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.000
The new architecture represents the current production baseline.

126
00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Mastering dependency triage is the standard for any team maintaining modern mobile applications.

